<Docs edit="site/src/pages/docs/concepts/components.astro" title="Components" description="The building blocks every Facet screen is made of, and what each one brings with it." group="Concepts" current={url('/docs/concepts/components/')} headings={headings}>
  <h2 id="what">What a component is</h2>
  <p>
    A component is one building block of the panel: a button, a toggle, a list row, a Home card, a chart, a clock face.
    Each one is drawn by Facet's renderer, written in C++ on <a href="https://lvgl.io">LVGL</a>, and takes every
    colour, size, gap and movement from the <a href={url('/docs/concepts/design-tokens/')}>design tokens</a>. Nothing on a
    screen is drawn by hand, so a button looks and behaves the same wherever it appears.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screens">Screens are made of components</h2>
  <p>
    A screen is a tree of components: a header, a layout, and the parts inside it. Screens are written as short JSON
    <em>specs</em> that name what to show and bind it to Home Assistant entities, not how to draw it. Positions, sizes
    and colours come from the components and the layout, so a spec stays the same on the 480 and the 720 panel.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also how you make your own screens: a new screen is a new arrangement of the components Facet already has.
    <a href={url('/ai/')}>Make screens with AI</a> shows how.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parts">What every component has</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Props</strong>: the values it shows, such as a label, a value or an icon. A prop can be fixed or follow an entity.</li>
    <li><strong>Variants</strong>: the looks it comes in, for example a primary, secondary or ghost button.</li>
    <li><strong>States</strong>: pressed, on, off, loading, unavailable and so on, from one shared set, so a disabled toggle looks like a disabled button.</li>
    <li><strong>Events and sounds</strong>: what it reports when you use it (a tap, a new value) and the sound it plays.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Updates</strong>: when something changes elsewhere, say a light switched on from your phone, the component
      moves to the new state with the same animation as a tap, without the sound.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every component also lists the colour pairs it uses, and each pair is tested for contrast in light and dark, so
    text stays readable whatever the theme.
  </p>

  <h2 id="library">Library and screen parts</h2>
  <p>
    Most components are the <strong>library</strong>: general parts any screen can use, including the ones you make. A
    few belong to one screen only, such as the parts of the Media or Calendar screen, and ship with that screen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="docs">Reading a component page</h2>
  <p>
    Every page under <a href={url('/components/')}>Components</a> draws the component live with the same renderer the
    panel runs, compiled for the browser, so what you see there is what the panel shows. Each page lists its variants,
    props, events and sounds, and the size switch shows it at 480 or 720.
  </p>
</Docs>
